const essentialCourses = {
word:{title:"Microsoft Word: Beginner to Professional Documents",intro:"Learn to create clear, accessible, well-structured documents for study, work and business.",modules:[
["Getting started with Word","A word processor creates and formats text documents. Learn the ribbon, tabs, document canvas, status bar, save/open, file formats and the difference between DOCX and PDF.","Create a new document, type a title and paragraph, save it as a DOCX, then export a PDF. Use a clear filename and a dedicated folder.","Deliverable: a one-page personal profile with a title, headings and readable paragraphs.","Which format is normally best for an editable Word document?","DOCX",["PDF","PNG"]],
["Text formatting & paragraph structure","Character formatting changes selected text: font, size, emphasis and colour. Paragraph formatting controls alignment, spacing, indentation, borders and line breaks. Use styles rather than manually formatting every heading.","Apply Title, Heading 1 and Heading 2 styles. Set readable font size, paragraph spacing and consistent alignment. Use bullets for unordered items and numbering for sequences.","Create a two-page study note with a clear heading hierarchy, bullet list and numbered procedure.","Why use built-in heading styles?","They create consistent structure and support navigation and accessibility",["They only change text colour","They automatically correct every fact"]],
["Page layout, sections & headers","Page setup controls margins, orientation, paper size and columns. Headers and footers repeat information. Section breaks allow different layouts or page numbering within one document.","Set margins, add a page number in the footer, create a section break and make one page landscape without changing the whole document.","Create a report with a cover page, portrait body and one landscape table page.","What does a section break allow?","Different layout or formatting settings within one document",["It deletes a page","It converts text to an image"]],
["Tables, images & references","Tables organise related information. Images need appropriate wrapping and alt text. Captions, cross-references, footnotes and citations help readers understand and verify a report.","Insert a table, resize columns, add a caption to an image, set alternative text and insert a footnote. Avoid using spaces to align columns.","Create a project report page containing a comparison table, labelled figure and footnote.","What should informative images include for accessibility?","Meaningful alternative text",["A decorative border only","A larger file size"]],
["Long documents & collaboration","Use navigation pane, styles, automatic table of contents, comments, track changes and version history. Use page and section breaks instead of repeated blank lines.","Apply heading styles to a multi-section report, insert an automatic table of contents, turn on Track Changes and respond to a comment.","Prepare a 5-section report template with automatic contents and review comments.","What powers an automatic table of contents?","Consistent heading styles",["Manual spaces","Text colour"]],
["Professional formatting & accessibility","Professional documents use consistent styles, clear language, descriptive links, adequate contrast, meaningful headings, accessible tables and correct metadata. Run spelling and accessibility checks, then inspect PDF export.","Use the accessibility checker, set document properties, inspect reading order and export a tagged PDF where available. Check page breaks and links after export.","Polish a CV or project report using consistent spacing, styles, page numbers and accessible structure.","What is a good final quality check?","Review layout, spelling, links, accessibility and exported PDF",["Only change the font","Assume export is always perfect"]],
["Workplace capstone","Create a complete professional report: cover, executive summary, contents, problem, method, findings, recommendations, references and appendix. Keep claims evidence-based and distinguish facts from assumptions.","Use a fictional project brief. Create the report, add tables and figures, use styles, insert contents and references, collaborate with comments, then export PDF.","Capstone: submit an editable DOCX and final PDF plus a short change log and source list.","Which pair is most useful to hand over?","Editable source document and final PDF",["Only screenshots","A file with no title or date"]]
]},
powerpoint:{title:"Microsoft PowerPoint: Beginner to Professional Presentations",intro:"Build presentations that explain ideas clearly, tell a coherent story and work reliably in classrooms, interviews and business meetings.",modules:[
["Interface, slides & slide layouts","A presentation is a sequence of visual pages supporting a message. Learn Normal view, Slide Sorter, Notes, layouts, placeholders, themes and presenter view.","Create a 5-slide deck using built-in layouts. Add a title, agenda, problem, evidence and conclusion. Save as PPTX.","Deliverable: a short introduction deck with consistent layouts.","What is the purpose of a slide layout?","It provides a consistent arrangement of content placeholders",["It writes the speech automatically","It compresses all images"]],
["Storytelling & slide structure","Start with the audience and the decision or understanding you want. Build a narrative: context, problem, evidence, insight, recommendation and next step. One main idea per slide improves clarity.","Turn a long paragraph into a headline plus three supporting points. Order slides so each answers the question raised by the previous one.","Create a 6-slide story explaining a problem and proposing a solution.","What should a strong slide communicate?","One clear main idea supported by relevant evidence",["Every detail from the report","Several unrelated messages"]],
["Typography, colour & visual hierarchy","Use readable type, consistent sizes, contrast, alignment, whitespace and a limited colour palette. Use emphasis to guide attention. Avoid tiny text and decorative clutter.","Create a title, section heading, body and footnote hierarchy. Align objects and use guides. Check contrast and readability from a distance.","Redesign a crowded slide into a clean visual with a clear focal point.","Why limit fonts and colours?","To improve consistency and reduce visual noise",["To make every slide identical","To hide missing information"]],
["Charts, diagrams & visual evidence","Choose a chart based on the question: line for trends, bar for comparisons, scatter for relationships. Label units, show sources and avoid misleading axes. Diagrams explain processes and relationships.","Convert a small sample dataset into a labelled bar chart. Add a source note and a one-sentence takeaway. Build a simple process diagram.","Create a data slide with a chart and a takeaway headline.","What is essential on a chart used for a decision?","Clear labels, units, source and honest scale",["3D effects","Unlabelled axes"]],
["Images, media & animation","Use licensed, relevant visuals. Crop consistently and provide alt text where appropriate. Animation should reveal sequence or explain change, not distract. Rehearse media playback and test on the presentation device.","Add one image with attribution, one simple process animation and speaker notes. Test playback and use animation sparingly.","Create a 3-step process slide with restrained transitions and notes.","When is animation useful?","When it clarifies sequence or directs attention",["On every object by default","To replace a clear explanation"]],
["Master slides, delivery & accessibility","Slide Master controls repeated elements, themes and layouts. Use meaningful reading order, accessible contrast, captions and notes. Rehearse timings, use presenter view and export a PDF backup.","Create a theme with master title/content layouts, consistent footer and accessible contrast. Rehearse a 3-minute talk and export PDF.","Prepare a reusable presentation template and a tested delivery copy.","What does Slide Master help manage?","Consistent global design and layouts",["Audience registration","Internet connectivity"]],
["Professional presentation capstone","A professional deck supports a talk: opening, context, evidence, analysis, recommendation, risks and call to action. Keep backup files and prepare to answer questions with evidence.","Build a 10-slide project or business proposal deck. Add speaker notes, sources, a clear conclusion and appendix for details. Rehearse and time it.","Capstone: deliver PPTX, PDF and a 3-minute spoken presentation with Q&A preparation.","What should you do before presenting on another computer?","Test fonts, media, links and backup PDF on the actual setup",["Assume everything will look identical","Remove all speaker notes"]]
]},
excel:{title:"Microsoft Excel: Beginner to Advanced Workplace Skills",intro:"Start with cells and formulas, then clean data, analyse it, build models and create reliable reports used in real workplaces.",modules:[
["Workbook, worksheets & cell basics","A workbook contains worksheets; a cell is identified by column and row such as B3. Learn ribbon, formula bar, ranges, selecting, entering data, fill handle, save and common formats.","Enter a small expense table with Date, Category, Description and Amount. Format the date and amount columns and rename the worksheet.","Create a clean monthly expense log with at least 10 fictional rows.","What does cell C5 identify?","Column C, row 5",["Column 5, row C","The fifth worksheet"]],
["Formulas, operators & references","Formulas begin with =. Use arithmetic operators and functions such as SUM, AVERAGE, MIN and MAX. Relative references adjust when copied; absolute references such as $B$2 stay fixed.","Enter =SUM(D2:D11), =AVERAGE(D2:D11), and use =$B$1*D2 to apply a fixed tax or rate cell. Copy the formula down and inspect references.","Build a bill calculator with quantity, unit price, subtotal, tax rate and total.","What does $B$2 mean in a formula?","An absolute reference that keeps column B and row 2 fixed",["A deleted cell","A text-only cell"]],
["Logic, text, dates & error handling","Use IF, AND, OR, IFERROR, COUNTIF, SUMIF, LEFT, RIGHT, TRIM, TEXT and date functions. Validate inputs and understand errors such as #N/A, #VALUE! and #DIV/0!.","Use =IF(D2>=1000,\"High\",\"Normal\"), =SUMIF(B:B,\"Food\",D:D), and TRIM to clean extra spaces. Test blank and invalid inputs.","Create a category summary and flag transactions above a threshold.","What does SUMIF do?","Adds values that meet a specified condition",["Sorts every worksheet","Deletes duplicate rows"]],
["Lookups & data cleaning","Use XLOOKUP where available, or INDEX/MATCH for compatibility. Clean data with TRIM, remove duplicates carefully, standardise dates and categories, and split columns when necessary. Never overwrite raw data without a backup.","Create a product table and use XLOOKUP to return a price by product ID. Keep a raw-data sheet and create a cleaned copy.","Clean a messy fictional customer/order list and map product codes to names.","Why preserve raw data?","So cleaning steps are reversible and original records remain available",["To make the workbook larger","Because formulas cannot use clean data"]],
["Sort, filter, tables & validation","Excel Tables expand with data and structured references. Sort and filter to inspect records. Data Validation restricts entries and dropdowns reduce inconsistent categories.","Convert a range to a table, filter by month and category, add a dropdown list for status and highlight overdue records.","Build a task or sales tracker with validation and conditional formatting.","What is a benefit of an Excel Table?","Structured references and automatic expansion as rows are added",["It prevents all data errors","It replaces backups"]],
["PivotTables, charts & dashboards","PivotTables summarise data by dimensions and measures. Choose suitable charts and create a dashboard with a few useful KPIs, filters and clear labels. Verify totals against source data.","Create a PivotTable showing sales by region and month. Add a bar chart and slicer if available. Reconcile grand totals with the source table.","Build a one-page sales dashboard with revenue, order count, regional comparison and trend.","What is a PivotTable mainly used for?","Summarising and exploring data across categories",["Writing long documents","Editing photographs"]],
["Power Query, modelling & professional controls","Power Query imports and transforms repeatable data. Learn data types, append/merge, refresh and query steps. Use named ranges, formula auditing, protection and documentation. Avoid fragile hard-coded assumptions.","Import a CSV with Power Query, change data types, remove blank rows and refresh after replacing the source file. Document assumptions and checks.","Create a refreshable monthly reporting workbook with separate raw, transform and report layers.","What is Power Query useful for?","Repeatable data import and transformation",["Animating charts","Replacing every formula"]],
["Excel professional capstone","Build a decision-ready model: clearly labelled inputs, calculations, outputs, checks, assumptions, scenario controls and a dashboard. Separate inputs from formulas and test edge cases.","Create a fictional small-business performance workbook with monthly revenue, costs, profit, budget variance, forecast assumptions, PivotTables and a dashboard.","Capstone: deliver the workbook, a one-page interpretation and a QA checklist. Explain limitations and how to refresh it.","What makes a spreadsheet model safer to use?","Clear inputs, documented assumptions, validation and independent checks",["Hidden formulas with no labels","Hard-coded numbers scattered everywhere"]]
]},
powerbi:{title:"Microsoft Power BI: Beginner to Professional Business Intelligence",intro:"Learn data analysis and reporting from zero: connect data, clean it, model relationships, write DAX measures and publish decision-ready reports.",modules:[
["BI concepts & Power BI Desktop","Business intelligence turns data into useful information for decisions. Learn report, dashboard, semantic model, Power BI Desktop, Power BI Service and the typical flow from source to report.","Install Power BI Desktop if available. Open a sample CSV, inspect fields and create a simple table visual. Save the PBIX project.","Deliverable: a first report page showing a few sample records.","What is the main purpose of business intelligence?","Turn data into information that supports decisions",["Store passwords","Replace every operational database"]],
["Connect, import & inspect data","Power BI can connect to files, databases and online services. Understand import versus DirectQuery at a high level, data types, column quality and source reliability. Keep credentials secure.","Import a CSV of fictional sales. Check column names, data types, blank values and duplicate IDs. Record the source and refresh assumptions.","Create a source inventory and a clean initial data preview.","Why should you inspect data types early?","Wrong types can break calculations, relationships and visuals",["They only change colours","They remove the need for validation"]],
["Power Query & data transformation","Power Query records repeatable transformation steps. Learn remove columns/rows, rename, split, replace, trim, change type, merge and append. Preserve source data and make refresh repeatable.","Clean a sample orders table: trim text, set date/currency types, remove blank rows and merge a product lookup. Review applied steps.","Deliver a refreshable cleaned dataset with documented transformations.","What does append do in Power Query?","Stacks rows from compatible tables",["Joins columns using a key","Creates a chart"]],
["Data modelling & relationships","A model connects fact tables to dimension tables. Learn primary/foreign keys, one-to-many relationships, filter direction, star schema and why duplicated data can create confusing results.","Create Sales fact, Date dimension, Product dimension and Region dimension. Connect keys and test totals before building visuals.","Draw a model diagram and verify one-to-many relationships.","What is a common recommended analytical model?","A star schema with fact and dimension tables",["One giant unstructured text column","A separate workbook for every chart"]],
["DAX measures & calculations","DAX is the formula language for measures and calculated columns. Learn SUM, COUNTROWS, DISTINCTCOUNT, DIVIDE, CALCULATE, filter context and row context. Prefer measures for aggregations that respond to report filters.","Create measures such as Total Sales = SUM(Sales[Amount]), Orders = COUNTROWS(Sales), and Average Order = DIVIDE([Total Sales],[Orders]). Compare with a calculated column.","Build KPI measures and validate each against a hand-calculated sample.","What does DIVIDE help with?","Safe division with optional handling for zero denominators",["Sorting text","Importing images"]],
["Visuals, filters & report design","Choose visuals based on the question. Use cards for KPIs, bars for comparisons, lines for trends and tables for detail. Learn slicers, visual/page/report filters, drill-through, tooltips and accessibility.","Build a report with KPI cards, monthly trend, sales by region and product comparison. Add a date slicer and descriptive titles.","Create a two-page report with overview and detail views.","Which visual is generally suitable for a time trend?","Line chart",["Pie chart with dozens of slices","Decorative word cloud"]],
["Publish, refresh, security & performance","Power BI Service supports publishing, sharing, refresh and workspaces subject to licence and tenant settings. Learn row-level security concepts, gateway basics, refresh failures and performance checks. Never publish confidential data publicly.","Publish only a safe sample report if you have access. Configure a refresh plan conceptually, test permissions and document who should see each page.","Write a deployment checklist covering data access, refresh, permissions and ownership.","What does row-level security control?","Which rows a user is allowed to see",["Which font is used","Whether a chart animates"]],
["Professional BI capstone & interview readiness","A professional BI project starts with stakeholder questions and metric definitions. Document grain, source, transformations, model, measures, validation and limitations. Prepare to explain why you chose each visual and measure.","Use a fictional retail dataset. Define KPIs, build a star schema, clean data, create DAX measures, design report pages, validate totals and prepare a 5-minute walkthrough.","Capstone: submit PBIX, PDF screenshots, model diagram, data dictionary, DAX notes, validation checklist and a short business recommendation.","What should you explain in a BI interview?","Business question, data model, transformations, measures, validation and insight",["Only the colour palette","Only how to open the file"]]
]},
interview:{title:"Job Interview & Career Readiness: Beginner to Confident Candidate",intro:"Learn how to find suitable roles, prove your skills, explain projects, answer behavioural and technical questions and prepare professionally.",modules:[
["Understand roles & job descriptions","A job description lists responsibilities, essential skills and preferred skills. Separate must-have requirements from nice-to-have items. Research the organisation and the actual work rather than applying by title alone.","Choose a sample job description. Highlight tools, core concepts, responsibilities, experience expectations and repeated keywords. Build a skill-gap table.","Deliverable: a role analysis with evidence you already have and gaps to close.","What should you do before tailoring an application?","Understand the role's responsibilities and required evidence",["Copy the same CV for every role","Claim every listed skill"]],
["CV, resume & application basics","A CV should be truthful, concise and relevant. Use clear sections, reverse chronology where appropriate, measurable outcomes and action verbs. Avoid fabricated metrics, misleading skill levels and unnecessary personal data.","Write a one-page entry-level CV with education, skills, projects, experience and links. For each project, state the problem, your contribution, tools and outcome.","Create a role-tailored CV and a short application note.","What makes a project bullet stronger?","Specific contribution, tools used and verifiable outcome",["A long list of buzzwords","Unverified claims"]],
["Portfolio & project storytelling","A portfolio demonstrates evidence. Each project should explain context, problem, your role, design or technical decisions, implementation, tests, limitations and next steps. Keep repositories reproducible.","Select one project. Improve README, setup instructions, screenshots, architecture diagram, test evidence and known limitations. Prepare a 90-second explanation.","Deliverable: one polished project case study with a live demo or clear screenshots.","What should a credible project case study include?","Problem, your contribution, process, evidence and limitations",["Only a logo","Only copied source code"]],
["Behavioural questions & STAR","Behavioural interviews assess how you acted in real situations. STAR means Situation, Task, Action and Result. Be specific about your own contribution and explain what you learned. Do not invent a story.","Prepare examples for teamwork, conflict, failure, initiative, learning quickly and meeting a deadline. Use STAR and keep the action section specific.","Create six concise STAR stories based on genuine experiences or clearly labelled academic examples.","In STAR, what does A represent?","Action you personally took",["Audience","Assumption"]],
["Technical interviews & problem solving","Technical interviews assess fundamentals and reasoning. Clarify the problem, state assumptions, consider edge cases, explain an approach, test it and discuss complexity or trade-offs where relevant. Think aloud clearly.","Practise explaining a basic concept, solving one role-relevant problem and testing boundary cases. If stuck, describe what you know and ask a clarifying question.","Build a 20-question technical revision sheet for your target role.","What is a good first step when a technical prompt is unclear?","Ask clarifying questions and state assumptions",["Immediately guess","Stay silent"]],
["Communication, group discussion & presentation","Speak clearly, structure answers and listen actively. In group discussions, contribute evidence, build on others' points and disagree respectfully. Presentations should have a clear opening, logical flow and conclusion.","Record a 2-minute introduction and a 3-minute project explanation. Review clarity, pace, filler words and whether your contribution is understandable.","Prepare an introduction, project pitch and one presentation with Q&A.","What is effective disagreement in a group discussion?","Respectfully explain reasoning and evidence while listening",["Interrupt repeatedly","Dismiss other views"]],
["HR, salary, ethics & workplace readiness","Prepare honest answers about motivation, strengths, learning areas and availability. Research market ranges carefully. Never share confidential data or misrepresent credentials. Ask about role expectations, mentorship and success measures.","Practise answers to why this role, strengths, a learning challenge, joining timeline and questions for the interviewer. Draft a professional follow-up message.","Create a one-page interview preparation brief for a real target role.","If asked about a skill you do not know, what is best?","Be honest, explain adjacent knowledge and how you would learn it",["Pretend to be an expert","Invent experience"]],
["Mock interviews, assessment & 30-60-90 plan","Practise under realistic time limits. Review answer quality, technical accuracy, clarity and evidence. After an interview, reflect on gaps and plan focused improvement. A course cannot guarantee selection.","Run a mock interview with a peer or recording. Score answers against a rubric: accuracy, structure, evidence, clarity and reflection. Create a 30-60-90 day learning plan.","Capstone: CV, portfolio, target-role analysis, six STAR stories, technical revision sheet, mock interview review and follow-up template.","What is the purpose of a mock interview review?","Find specific gaps and improve the next attempt",["Guarantee a job offer","Memorise one script for every question"]]
]}
 
,outlook:{title:"Microsoft Outlook & Teams: Professional Communication",intro:"Manage professional email, calendars, meetings and collaboration with clear, secure workplace habits.",modules:[
["Email foundations & inbox","Understand addresses, subject lines, To/CC/BCC, attachments, signatures, folders and conversation threads. Email is a record; write for clarity and action.","Compose a concise email with a descriptive subject, greeting, context, request, deadline and sign-off. Attach a non-sensitive sample file.","Deliverable: a professional email template and organised inbox folders.","When should BCC be used?","When recipients should not see one another's addresses",["To hide the subject","To attach a large file"]],
["Writing, etiquette & follow-up","Use concise paragraphs, one clear ask, appropriate tone and explicit deadlines. Verify recipients and attachments. Avoid replying-all unnecessarily and never send confidential information to the wrong audience.","Rewrite a vague email into a clear action request. Draft a polite follow-up and an out-of-office reply.","Create templates for request, update, meeting follow-up and escalation.","What makes an email actionable?","Clear context, owner, requested action and due date",["Longer paragraphs","A subject containing only 'Hi'"]],
["Calendar & time management","Create events, invite attendees, use time zones, availability, recurrence and reminders. Include an agenda and meeting purpose. Respect focus time and scheduling boundaries.","Schedule a fictional project meeting with agenda, attendees, duration, time zone and pre-read.","Deliverable: a one-week calendar plan with focus blocks and meeting buffers.","What should a useful meeting invitation include?","Purpose, agenda, time, attendees and preparation",["Only a title","No time zone for a global team"]],
["Meetings & Teams collaboration","Learn channels, chats, calls, meeting roles, screen sharing, file collaboration and meeting notes. Keep decisions and actions discoverable in the right team space.","Create a meeting agenda, take notes and publish decisions, action owners and deadlines in a shared location.","Prepare a meeting notes template and action tracker.","Where should durable team decisions be recorded?","In an agreed shared workspace or project record",["Only in a private chat","Only in someone's memory"]],
["Files, security & privacy","Check sharing permissions, verify recipients, use approved storage and recognise phishing, spoofed senders, suspicious links and unexpected attachments. Follow organisational retention and data rules.","Review a simulated suspicious email. Identify sender mismatch, urgency, unexpected attachment and unsafe link. Report rather than interact.","Create a safe email and file-sharing checklist.","What should you do with a suspicious attachment?","Do not open it; report it through the approved security process",["Forward it to everyone","Download it to inspect"]],
["Professional workflow capstone","Combine email, calendar and team collaboration into a clear workflow: request, schedule, meeting, decisions, actions and follow-up.","Run a simulated project kickoff: send invitation, circulate agenda, record decisions, assign actions and send a concise follow-up.","Capstone: a complete communication pack with email thread, calendar plan, meeting notes and action log.","What demonstrates professional communication?","Clear, timely, respectful messages with traceable actions",["Frequent messages without purpose","Using reply-all for every update"]]
]},
access:{title:"Microsoft Access: Relational Database Foundations",intro:"Learn tables, keys, relationships, queries, forms and reports by building a small structured database.",modules:[
["Database thinking & Access interface","A database stores structured records. Learn tables, fields, records, data types, primary keys, queries, forms and reports. Distinguish a database from a spreadsheet.","Create a blank practice database and identify where tables, queries, forms and reports appear.","Deliverable: a database plan for a small library or inventory system.","What uniquely identifies a row in a table?","A primary key",["A report title","A cell colour"]],
["Tables, fields & data types","Design tables around entities. Choose appropriate data types, required fields, validation rules and unique identifiers. Avoid storing multiple values in one field.","Create Customers and Orders tables with IDs, dates, amounts and text fields. Add required-field and validation rules.","Create a data dictionary explaining each field and its type.","Why use appropriate data types?","To validate values and support correct sorting and calculations",["To change the logo","To remove all relationships"]],
["Relationships & normalisation","Relational databases link tables using primary and foreign keys. Learn one-to-many relationships, referential integrity and basic normalisation to reduce duplicated facts.","Connect Customers.CustomerID to Orders.CustomerID. Enable referential integrity and test an invalid orphan record.","Draw a simple ER diagram and explain the relationship cardinality.","What does referential integrity help prevent?","Invalid references to records that do not exist",["All duplicate report titles","Slow typing"]],
["Queries & criteria","Queries retrieve and combine records. Learn filters, criteria, sorting, calculated fields, joins, totals and parameter prompts. Make changes carefully when using update/delete queries.","Build a query for orders above a threshold and a totals query grouped by customer. Preview records before changing data.","Create three saved queries for common business questions.","What should you do before running a delete query?","Back up data and verify the selection criteria",["Run it on the only copy","Hide the table"]],
["Forms, reports & usability","Forms provide controlled data entry; reports format output for review or printing. Use labels, validation, clear navigation and meaningful grouping.","Build a customer entry form and a grouped order report with totals. Test invalid and missing input.","Create a small front end with a form and printable report.","What is the main purpose of a form?","To make data entry and editing easier and more controlled",["To replace database backups","To run operating system updates"]],
["Security, maintenance & capstone","Understand file protection limits, backups, compact/repair, split database concepts and multi-user limitations. Access is useful for small desktop database scenarios, not every enterprise workload.","Create a small inventory database with Products, Suppliers and Purchases. Add relationships, queries, form, report and backup copy.","Capstone: submit database, ER diagram, data dictionary, test cases and user instructions.","What is an essential maintenance practice?","Regular verified backups",["Keep one copy only","Share the database publicly"]]
]},
powerautomate:{title:"Microsoft Power Automate: Workflow Automation Foundations",intro:"Automate repetitive work with triggers, actions, conditions, approvals and safe error handling. Features vary by licence and organisation.",modules:[
["Automation mindset & flow types","Automation connects events to actions. Learn cloud flows, desktop flows, triggers, actions, connectors and when a simple checklist is safer than automation.","Map a repetitive low-risk task into trigger, conditions, actions and expected result.","Deliverable: a workflow diagram before building anything.","What starts an automated flow?","A trigger",["A dashboard colour","A file extension"]],
["Build your first cloud flow","Learn templates, manual/automated/scheduled triggers, connectors and dynamic content. Start with sample data and least-privilege access.","Create a simple flow that sends a notification when a sample form response arrives, if your environment permits.","Document trigger, input fields, action and expected notification.","What is dynamic content?","Data from earlier trigger/action steps that can be used later",["A random password","A slide animation"]],
["Conditions, variables & loops","Use conditions for branching, variables for values, apply-to-each for collections and expressions for transformations. Keep flows readable and avoid unnecessary nested logic.","Build a flow that routes a sample request based on amount or category and records the decision.","Create a flow diagram with normal and exception paths.","What does a condition do?","Chooses a path based on whether an expression is true or false",["Deletes the trigger","Changes a user's licence"]],
["Approvals & business workflows","Approvals support review and decision processes. Define approvers, timeouts, escalation, rejection paths and audit trail. Never automate high-impact decisions without appropriate human oversight.","Design a leave or purchase request approval with submitter, approver, decision, comments and notification.","Deliverable: approval process map and test cases.","Why should approval flows include rejection handling?","So declined requests have a clear outcome and communication",["To hide decisions","To bypass reviewers"]],
["Errors, monitoring & security","Use run history, scopes, configure-run-after, retries and notifications. Protect credentials, review connector permissions and avoid exposing personal or confidential data in logs.","Simulate a failed action and document how to diagnose it. Add a failure notification and safe retry policy.","Create a support runbook for the flow.","What should you avoid putting in flow logs?","Passwords, secrets and unnecessary sensitive data",["A generic flow name","A non-sensitive status"]],
["Professional automation capstone","Choose a repeatable low-risk workflow, document current effort, design triggers and actions, test edge cases, assign an owner and define a manual fallback.","Build or prototype a request tracker that captures input, validates it, routes approval, records status and notifies stakeholders.","Capstone: flow export or screenshots, architecture, permissions, tests, failure handling and maintenance owner.","What makes automation production-ready?","Testing, security, monitoring, ownership and fallback procedures",["It runs once successfully","No documentation"]]
]},
git:{title:"Git & GitHub: Version Control and Collaboration",intro:"Learn to save code history, work with branches, collaborate through pull requests and maintain a professional repository.",modules:[
["Files, repositories & first commit","Git tracks changes over time. A repository contains project files and history. Learn working directory, staging area, commit, remote and the difference between Git and GitHub.","Install Git or use a guided environment. Configure name/email, initialise a practice repo, add README and make first commit.","Deliverable: a repository with a clear README and first commit.","What does a commit represent?","A recorded snapshot of staged project changes",["A website deployment only","A folder colour"]],
["Status, add, commit & history","Use git status, add, commit, log and diff. Write meaningful commit messages. Stage only intended files and inspect changes before committing.","Edit README, inspect git diff, stage the change, commit it and review git log.","Create a small history with focused commits.","Why run git status before committing?","To understand changed, staged and untracked files",["To delete branches","To publish a website"]],
["Branches & merging","Branches allow isolated work. Learn checkout/switch, branch, merge and conflict resolution. Keep changes small and integrate frequently.","Create a feature branch, edit a file, commit, switch back and merge. Resolve a simple conflict in a practice repository.","Deliverable: a feature branch merged into main.","What is a branch useful for?","Working on a change without immediately altering the main line",["Encrypting a repository","Compressing images"]],
["GitHub remotes & pull requests","A remote connects local Git to a hosted repository. Learn clone, fetch, pull, push, forks, pull requests, review comments and merge strategies.","Push a practice branch to GitHub and open a pull request. Add a clear description, test evidence and checklist.","Create a pull request with one reviewer-style checklist.","What is a pull request for?","Proposing and reviewing changes before integration",["Changing your computer password","Creating a spreadsheet"]],
["Conflicts, ignore files & security","Resolve conflicts by understanding both changes. Use .gitignore for generated files and local secrets. Never commit API keys, passwords, private datasets or personal records. Rotate exposed secrets immediately.","Create a .gitignore for build outputs and local environment files. Practise a conflict. Scan repository history for accidental secrets in a safe demo.","Prepare a repository hygiene checklist.","Should .env files with secrets be committed?","No; keep secrets out of version control",["Yes, if the repo is private","Only on Fridays"]],
["Professional repository & collaboration","Use issues, milestones, README, licence awareness, releases, tags, actions and contribution guidance. Keep commits reviewable and documentation reproducible.","Improve a project repo with setup steps, usage, screenshots, issue template, release notes and a basic test workflow.","Capstone: a polished portfolio repository and pull request workflow.","What makes a repository easier for others to use?","Clear README, reproducible setup, licence clarity and tested instructions",["A very long repo name","No documentation"]]
]}
,
logic:{title:"Computational Thinking & Programming Logic: Absolute Zero",intro:"Start without assuming you know what code is. Learn how to think through a problem, express steps clearly, trace decisions and turn everyday reasoning into algorithms.",modules:[
["What is a computer and what is code?","A computer follows instructions. A program is a precise set of instructions that transforms input into output. A programmer describes the steps and checks whether the result is correct. Learn hardware, software, operating systems, applications, files and the role of a programming language.","Example: making tea can be described as inputs (water, tea, milk), steps (boil, steep, mix) and output (a cup of tea). Computers need instructions that are much more exact than human instructions.","Write steps for brushing teeth and making a cup of tea. Identify input, process, output and one possible failure.","What is a program?","A set of precise instructions a computer can execute",["A computer screen","A type of keyboard"]],
["Problem solving: input, process, output","Before writing code, restate the problem. Identify what information is given, what result is required, rules and constraints. Split a large task into small steps. This is decomposition.","Problem: calculate total price. Input: item price and quantity. Process: multiply them. Output: total. Check whether quantity can be zero or negative.","For a student result calculator, list inputs, processing steps, outputs and at least three validation rules.","Why identify inputs and outputs first?","It makes the required transformation and expected result clear",["It chooses a font","It removes the need to test"]],
["Algorithms and precision","An algorithm is a finite, ordered set of unambiguous steps that solves a problem. A good algorithm has clear inputs, outputs, termination and correctness. The same problem can have multiple algorithms.","Algorithm to find the larger of two numbers: read A and B; if A is greater, display A; otherwise display B. Decide how equal values should be handled.","Write an algorithm to find the largest of three numbers. Include the equal-number case.","What makes an algorithm unambiguous?","Each step has one clear interpretation",["It uses difficult words","It must be written in Python"]],
["Flowcharts and pseudocode","Pseudocode describes logic in readable structured language without strict programming syntax. Flowcharts show control flow visually: oval for start/end, parallelogram for input/output, rectangle for process and diamond for decisions.","Pseudocode: INPUT age; IF age >= 18 THEN PRINT Adult ELSE PRINT Minor. A flowchart uses a decision diamond with Yes and No branches.","Write pseudocode and draw a flowchart for checking whether a number is even or odd.","What is pseudocode useful for?","Planning and checking logic before choosing a language",["Replacing all testing","Executing directly on a CPU"]],
["Variables, values and data types","A variable is a named place to refer to a value. Common types include integers, decimal numbers, text, true/false values and collections. Choose types that match the meaning of the data. Assignment stores or updates a value.","age = 21; price = 49.5; name = \"Asha\"; is_student = True. Predict each value and type. Distinguish assignment from mathematical equality.","Create a variable list for a shopping bill: item name, quantity, price, discount and final total.","Which type best represents a yes/no condition?","Boolean",["String","Floating-point number"]],
["Operators and expressions","Arithmetic operators calculate values; comparison operators produce true/false results; logical operators combine conditions. Precedence determines evaluation order, so use parentheses when they improve clarity.","For price=100 and quantity=3, total=price*quantity. A discount applies when total>=250 AND member is true. Trace each part.","Evaluate five expressions by hand. Include brackets, division, comparison and AND/OR/NOT.","What does a comparison such as x >= 10 produce?","A Boolean true/false result",["A text string","A loop"]],
["Decisions and branching","A conditional chooses which path runs based on a Boolean condition. IF handles a condition; ELSE handles the alternative; ELSE IF handles multiple cases. Conditions should cover all intended cases without accidental gaps.","Grade logic: if score>=90 A; else if score>=75 B; else if score>=40 C; else fail. Test scores 39, 40, 74, 75, 89, 90 and 100.","Design an eligibility checker with at least three conditions. Test boundary values and explain the chosen order.","Why test boundary values?","Errors often occur where a condition changes from true to false",["They make code run faster","They replace normal test cases"]],
["Loops and repetition","Loops repeat instructions. A counted loop is useful when the number of repetitions is known. A condition-controlled loop continues while a condition holds. Every loop needs progress toward termination to avoid an infinite loop.","Repeat adding 1 through 5 to a sum. Trace the changing counter and sum in a table. Compare this with repeating until the user enters a valid value.","Write pseudocode to print 1 to 10, calculate their sum and validate a positive input. Trace each iteration.","What can cause an infinite loop?","The loop condition never becomes false",["A variable has a name","A program has comments"]],
["Functions and modular thinking","A function is a named reusable block that performs a focused task. Parameters provide inputs; a return value gives a result. Small functions make programs easier to test, understand and maintain.","function area(width,height) returns width*height. Calling area(4,3) returns 12. Separate calculation from input and display.","Design three functions for a bill calculator: calculate subtotal, calculate discount and calculate final total.","Why use functions?","To reuse and isolate logic into testable units",["To avoid naming variables","To make every program longer"]],
["Arrays, strings and collections","Programs often process groups of related values. Arrays/lists store ordered items; strings store text; dictionaries/maps associate keys with values. Learn indexing, iteration, length and common off-by-one mistakes.","For marks=[60,75,90], index positions may be 0,1,2 in many languages. Average is sum divided by count. Never assume the index starts at one.","Find the minimum, maximum and average of a list. Explain what should happen for an empty list.","What is an off-by-one error?","Using an index or loop boundary one step too far or too little",["A spelling mistake","A network failure"]],
["Debugging and tracing","Debugging is a disciplined process: reproduce the issue, read the error, isolate the smallest failing case, inspect values, form a hypothesis, change one thing and retest. Trace tables reveal how variables change line by line.","Trace x=2; repeat 3 times: x=x*2. Record x after each iteration: 4, 8, 16. Compare expected and actual output.","Fix a deliberately incorrect algorithm for finding the largest list item. Write the failing input before changing it.","What is the best first step when debugging?","Reproduce the problem and understand the observed failure",["Rewrite everything","Delete the test"]],
["Testing, edge cases and correctness","A test checks whether behaviour matches requirements. Use normal cases, boundary cases, invalid inputs and edge cases. Expected results should be written before running tests. Passing tests increases confidence but cannot prove every possible case.","For a divide function test 10/2, 0/5, negative values and division by zero. Define the expected behaviour for zero divisor.","Create a test table for your eligibility checker with input, expected output, actual output and pass/fail.","Why include edge cases?","They expose failures outside the ordinary happy path",["They make the interface colourful","They reduce documentation"]],
["From logic to your first program","Translate pseudocode into a language only after the logic is clear. Learn the edit-run-observe-debug cycle. Read syntax errors carefully; compare output with the expected result. Code is a way to express an algorithm, not a substitute for understanding it.","Convert the even/odd algorithm into Python: n=int(input()); if n%2==0 print Even else print Odd. Explain modulo and indentation.","Build a menu-driven calculator with addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, input validation and at least eight test cases.","What should come before language-specific syntax?","Understanding the problem and designing the algorithm",["Choosing an IDE theme","Copying a finished solution"]]
]},
dsa:{title:"Data Structures & Algorithms: Zero to Interview Ready",intro:"Learn how data is stored and how problems are solved efficiently. Start with complexity and arrays, then build the structures, algorithms, patterns and interview habits used in software roles.",modules:[
["What are data structures and algorithms?","A data structure organises information; an algorithm transforms or searches it. The right choice affects speed, memory and clarity. Learn the difference between an abstract idea and a language-specific implementation.","A contact list may use an array for ordered display, a hash map for fast lookup and a tree for sorted navigation. Each choice optimises a different operation.","For a student directory, list operations (search, insert, delete, sort) and choose a simple structure for each.","What does a data structure decide?","How data is organised and accessed",["The programming language colour","The monitor resolution"]],
["Time and space complexity","Complexity describes how resource use grows with input size n. Big-O focuses on growth, not exact seconds. Common classes: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), O(2ⁿ). Space complexity tracks extra memory.","A loop over n items is O(n). Two nested loops over n items are usually O(n²). Binary search halves a sorted search space: O(log n).","Classify five snippets by counting operations. Explain why 2n+10 is O(n) and n²+3n is O(n²).","What does O(n) mean?","Work grows roughly in proportion to input size",["It always takes one second","It means no memory is used"]],
["Arrays and strings","Arrays store indexed elements, often contiguously. Access by index is O(1); searching is O(n); inserting in the middle may require shifting O(n) items. Strings are sequences of characters with language-specific immutability rules.","Trace two pointers from both ends to test a palindrome. For [4,7,2], index 1 returns 7 in constant time.","Solve: find maximum, reverse an array, count vowels, check palindrome and remove duplicates. State time and space complexity.","What is typical array index access complexity?","O(1)",["O(n²)","O(2ⁿ)"]],
["Linked lists, stacks and queues","Linked lists connect nodes with references; access by position is O(n), while insertion after a known node can be O(1). A stack is last-in-first-out; a queue is first-in-first-out.","Browser back history can be modelled as a stack. A printer queue processes jobs in arrival order. Draw node links and trace push/pop/enqueue/dequeue.","Implement stack operations, queue operations and reverse a linked list conceptually. Test empty and one-element cases.","Which structure follows FIFO?","Queue",["Stack","Binary search tree"]],
["Hash maps and sets","A hash map stores key-value pairs and uses a hash function to locate buckets. Average lookup/insert is often O(1), but collisions and poor hashing matter. A set stores unique values.","Count word frequencies using a map: for each word, increment count[word]. Use a set to detect duplicates while scanning.","Solve two-sum, first non-repeating character and duplicate detection. Explain average-case assumptions.","What is a set especially useful for?","Tracking unique values and membership",["Keeping values always sorted","Representing a call stack"]],
["Recursion and backtracking","Recursion solves a problem by calling itself on smaller inputs. Every recursive function needs a base case and progress toward it. Backtracking explores choices and reverses a choice when a path fails.","Factorial: fact(n)=1 when n<=1, otherwise n*fact(n-1). Trace fact(4). Draw the call stack and return values.","Solve factorial, sum of digits and generate subsets. Identify base case, recursive step and stack depth.","Why is a base case essential?","It stops recursion from continuing indefinitely",["It sorts the input","It allocates a database"]],
["Searching and sorting","Linear search scans items; binary search requires sorted data and halves the search interval. Sorting orders data. Learn selection, insertion, merge and quick sort, their trade-offs and stability.","Binary search in [2,5,8,12,16,23,38] for 16: inspect middle, discard half, repeat. Merge sort divides, sorts and merges.","Implement linear and binary search; trace insertion sort and merge sort on a small list. Compare complexity.","What precondition does binary search require?","The search range must be sorted",["All values must be positive","The list must contain exactly ten values"]],
["Trees, binary search trees and heaps","Trees model hierarchy. A binary tree has up to two children per node. A BST orders left and right subtrees under a defined duplicate policy. Heaps support efficient min/max retrieval; a priority queue uses this idea.","Draw a BST by inserting 8,3,10,1,6. Trace inorder traversal (sorted output) and preorder traversal.","Implement traversals, search in a BST and use a heap to find the k largest values.","Which traversal of a valid BST returns sorted keys?","Inorder",["Preorder","Level order"]],
["Graphs and traversal","Graphs contain vertices and edges, directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted. Represent them with adjacency lists or matrices. BFS explores layers using a queue; DFS explores deeply using recursion or a stack.","Model cities as vertices and roads as edges. BFS from one city finds minimum edge-count distance in an unweighted graph.","Build an adjacency list and trace BFS and DFS. Solve connected components and shortest path in an unweighted graph.","Which structure is commonly used by BFS?","Queue",["Stack only","Hash function"]],
["Greedy, dynamic programming and patterns","Greedy algorithms make locally optimal choices and need a correctness argument. Dynamic programming stores overlapping subproblem results using memoisation or tabulation. Learn two pointers, sliding window, prefix sums and binary search on answer.","Fibonacci recursion repeats work; memoisation caches results. A sliding window tracks a moving contiguous range without recomputing every element.","Solve maximum subarray, longest substring without repeats, coin change and climbing stairs. Explain why the chosen pattern fits.","What makes dynamic programming useful?","Overlapping subproblems and reusable optimal substructure",["Every problem has sorted input","It avoids all loops"]],
["Complexity trade-offs and interview problem solving","Interview work includes clarifying requirements, examples, brute force, optimisation, correctness reasoning, complexity, clean implementation and tests. Communicate while solving. Do not jump straight to code.","For two-sum, begin with nested loops O(n²), then use a hash map for average O(n). Explain the memory trade-off and duplicate handling.","Solve a problem aloud using: clarify, examples, brute force, pattern, implementation, tests, complexity and improvement.","What should you do before coding an interview problem?","Clarify inputs, outputs, constraints and examples",["Guess the intended solution","Start typing without a plan"]],
["DSA capstone and revision plan","Combine structures and patterns in a small searchable task manager or route planner. Document operations, complexity, edge cases and tests. Revisit weak patterns using spaced practice rather than memorising solutions.","A task manager may use a hash map by ID, a heap for priorities and a graph for dependencies. Explain why each structure fits.","Build a command-line contact/task manager and solve 20 progressively harder problems. Record mistakes and explain each solution without looking.","What is strong evidence of DSA learning?","Solving unfamiliar problems and explaining correctness and trade-offs",["Memorising code only","Watching videos without practice"]]
]},
vibecoding:{title:"AI-Assisted & Vibe Coding: From Prompt to Reliable Software",intro:"Use AI coding assistants to plan, generate, understand, test and improve software. Learn prompt design, repository context, debugging and security. AI output must be reviewed; never trust generated code blindly.",modules:[
["What vibe coding is — and is not","Vibe coding is an informal approach where natural-language prompts guide AI tools to generate code. It can speed up prototypes, but it does not remove the need to understand requirements, review code, test behaviour or protect data.","Ask an assistant to create a simple to-do page. Compare a vague prompt with a prompt specifying users, features, constraints and acceptance criteria.","Write a one-page product brief before asking AI to code. Define the user, problem, features and non-goals.","What is the biggest risk of blind AI-generated code?","It may be incorrect, insecure or unsuitable while appearing convincing",["It always runs too slowly","It cannot contain syntax"]],
["Choose the right tool and workflow","Understand editor-based assistants, chat-based coding, browser builders and agentic tools. Compare repository access, terminal access, model usage, privacy, pricing, exportability and review controls. Features change over time.","A chat assistant can explain a function; an IDE assistant can edit files with context; an agent may run tests. Keep a human approval step for consequential changes.","Compare two available tools using a checklist: cost, privacy, language support, repository integration and ability to inspect diffs.","Why inspect an AI tool's permissions?","To understand what code, files and commands it can access",["To change syntax highlighting","To increase monitor brightness"]],
["Prompting with requirements","A strong coding prompt states role, context, goal, existing stack, constraints, desired output, acceptance tests and what not to change. Ask for a plan before implementation on larger tasks.","Prompt pattern: Context; Goal; Current behaviour; Expected behaviour; Constraints; Files allowed; Acceptance criteria; Test commands; Report changes and limitations.","Turn a vague request ('make a website') into a precise specification with user stories and measurable acceptance criteria.","What makes an acceptance criterion useful?","It describes an observable condition that can be tested",["It says 'make it beautiful' only","It hides the expected behaviour"]],
["Plan before generating code","Break work into small tasks. Ask AI to inspect existing files and summarise architecture before edits. Agree on a plan, identify dependencies and create a minimal first milestone. Avoid giant prompts that change everything at once.","For a login bug, first request diagnosis and evidence, then a minimal patch, then tests. Review the diff after each step.","Create a task list for a small expense tracker. Mark dependencies and define the first vertical slice.","Why make small changes?","They are easier to review, test and roll back",["They guarantee no bugs","They eliminate the need for Git"]],
["Understand generated code","Read the diff, identify entry points, data flow, dependencies, error paths and assumptions. Ask the assistant to explain unfamiliar code line by line, then verify explanations against documentation and execution.","Ask: 'Trace what happens from button click to database write. Identify validation, error handling and data permissions.'","Pick a generated function and write its input/output contract, edge cases and plain-language explanation.","Should you commit code you cannot explain?","Not until you understand and review its behaviour",["Yes, if AI wrote it","Only if the file is short"]],
["Debug with evidence","Provide the exact error, reproduction steps, expected/actual behaviour, relevant code and environment. Ask for hypotheses and tests before accepting a fix. Avoid pasting secrets or private data.","Good bug report: steps, expected result, actual result, console error, recent change and minimal reproduction. Test the fix against the original failure.","Create a minimal reproduction for a bug and ask AI for three hypotheses with evidence needed to distinguish them.","What helps AI debug accurately?","A reproducible case and exact error/context",["A vague 'it is broken'","A screenshot with passwords"]],
["Testing and code review","Require unit, integration and end-to-end tests appropriate to the risk. Review edge cases, accessibility, performance and regression. AI-generated tests can share the same mistaken assumptions as generated code.","For a calculator, test normal values, zero, negative values, invalid input and division by zero. Review whether tests assert meaningful outcomes.","Ask AI to propose tests, then independently verify the expected results and add a regression test.","Why are AI-generated tests not enough by themselves?","They can repeat the same misunderstanding as the generated implementation",["They cannot run in a browser","They always use too much memory"]],
["Security, privacy and licensing","Never expose API keys, passwords, tokens, private student data or customer records in prompts or public repos. Review dependencies, permissions, injection risks, data handling and licence obligations. Use environment variables and least privilege.","A generated app that puts a secret key in frontend JavaScript exposes it to visitors. Move secrets to a trusted backend and rotate any exposed key.","Perform a security review of a sample app: secrets, input validation, authentication, authorisation, dependency risk and data minimisation.","Where should a private API secret live?","In a protected server-side secret store or environment configuration",["In public frontend JavaScript","In a public GitHub README"]],
["Git, checkpoints and safe agent work","Use branches and commits before risky changes. Ask agents to work on a branch, inspect every diff, run tests and report changed files. Keep backups and know how to revert. Limit tool permissions.","Workflow: create branch; record baseline; ask for one change; inspect diff; run tests; commit; merge after review.","Practise a branch-based AI task and write a change summary with tests, risks and rollback instructions.","What is a checkpoint useful for?","Restoring a known-good state after a bad change",["Replacing all tests","Publishing credentials"]],
["Deploy, monitor and maintain","A prototype is not production-ready. Learn environment configuration, build steps, deployment previews, logs, error monitoring, accessibility, backups and rollback. Monitor real behaviour and fix regressions.","Deploy a static demo to a preview environment, check mobile layout, inspect console/network errors and document rollback.","Create a deployment checklist and a post-release test plan for your project.","What should happen after deployment?","Verify behaviour, monitor issues and retain a rollback path",["Assume the preview guarantees production","Delete the source repository"]],
["Build a complete AI-assisted project","Choose a small real problem. Write requirements, design a simple architecture, implement one slice at a time, review code, test edge cases, document setup and deploy. Record which parts AI assisted and what you verified.","Example capstone: a personal study planner with local persistence, input validation, filters, accessible controls and an export feature. Avoid storing sensitive data.","Deliver a repository with README, requirements, screenshots, tests, known limitations, setup instructions and a short explanation of every major component.","What proves responsible AI-assisted development?","A tested, reviewed project you can explain and maintain",["The number of prompts used","A large amount of generated code"]]
]}
,

business:{title:"Business & Accounting Foundations",intro:"Learn how organisations create value, record transactions, understand performance and improve operations. Use fictional examples; this is an educational foundation, not professional accounting or tax advice.",modules:[
["How a business works","A business creates value for a customer and receives revenue in return. It uses people, materials, technology and capital. Revenue is not the same as profit: costs must be deducted. Learn customers, products, value proposition, stakeholders and business models.","Example: a shop sells 10 items at ₹100 each. Revenue = 10 × ₹100 = ₹1,000. If the goods cost ₹600 and other costs are ₹200, simplified profit = ₹1,000 − ₹600 − ₹200 = ₹200.","Choose a fictional small business. Describe its customer, problem, offer, revenue source, main costs and one risk.","What is the difference between revenue and profit?","Profit is what remains after relevant costs are deducted",["Revenue is always the cash in the bank","They mean exactly the same thing"],"A business can have high revenue and still lose money if its costs are higher."],
["Bookkeeping and the accounting equation","Bookkeeping records transactions consistently. The accounting equation is Assets = Liabilities + Equity. A transaction changes at least two accounts in a balanced way. Learn source documents, ledgers, debit/credit as a system and reconciliation.","A business owner invests ₹50,000 cash. Assets (cash) rise ₹50,000 and equity rises ₹50,000. The equation remains balanced: 50,000 = 0 + 50,000.","Create five fictional transactions and show which asset, liability, income, expense or equity accounts change.","What must remain balanced in the basic accounting equation?","Assets = Liabilities + Equity",["Revenue = Assets + Expenses","Cash = Profit every month"],"The equation is the foundation of double-entry accounting."],
["Read the three financial statements","The income statement summarises revenue and expenses over a period. The balance sheet shows assets, liabilities and equity at a date. The cash-flow statement groups cash movement into operating, investing and financing activities. Profit and cash flow can differ.","A fictional company reports ₹20,000 profit but customers still owe ₹8,000. Profit may be positive while cash collection is delayed; receivables explain part of the difference.","Prepare a one-page simplified income statement and balance sheet from a small fictional transaction set. Label period vs point-in-time figures.","Which statement shows assets and liabilities at a particular date?","Balance sheet",["Income statement","Marketing plan"],"The balance sheet is a snapshot at a date."],
["Costs, margin and unit economics","Fixed costs do not change directly with short-run output; variable costs move with units. Contribution per unit = selling price − variable cost per unit. Break-even units = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit, when contribution is positive.","A product sells for ₹500 and variable cost is ₹300. Contribution = ₹200. With fixed costs ₹10,000, break-even = 10,000 ÷ 200 = 50 units.","Build a spreadsheet for three price/cost scenarios. Calculate contribution, gross margin and break-even; explain which assumptions matter most.","If contribution per unit is zero or negative, what happens to the simple break-even formula?","There is no finite positive break-even from sales at that contribution",["Break-even is always one unit","Fixed costs become zero"],"A positive contribution is required to recover fixed costs through unit sales."],
["Market research and customer understanding","Market research reduces uncertainty. Separate observations, interview evidence, assumptions and opinions. Define a segment, customer problem, alternatives and willingness-to-pay questions. Avoid leading questions and do not treat a tiny sample as representative.","A neutral interview asks: 'Tell me about the last time you tried to solve this problem.' A leading question asks: 'Wouldn't our app make this easier?' The first invites evidence without suggesting the answer.","Write a short research plan with a target segment, five neutral questions, consent/privacy notes and a way to summarise themes.","Why avoid leading questions?","They can bias responses and distort evidence",["They make interviews shorter","They guarantee a larger sample"],"Good research tries to discover what is true, not confirm what the researcher hopes."],
["Operations, controls and business recommendation","Operations turn inputs into repeatable products or services. Map a process, identify bottlenecks, define service measures and controls. A useful recommendation states the problem, evidence, options, costs, risks, owner and success measure.","A fictional order process takes 2 days in approval and 1 day in fulfilment. The approval stage is the largest delay, so investigate it before automating the whole process.","Create a process map and a one-page improvement memo with baseline metric, proposed change, risk, owner and 30-day review measure.","What makes a recommendation actionable?","A clear action, owner, evidence, risk and success measure",["A colourful title only","A target with no baseline"],"Decision-makers need to know what changes, who owns it and how results will be checked."],
]},
it:{title:"IT Support, Networking & Cloud Foundations",intro:"Build practical foundations in computer systems, operating systems, networks, cloud identity and defensive security. Use isolated training environments and never test systems without permission.",modules:[
["Computer systems and troubleshooting","A computer combines hardware, firmware, operating system and applications. Troubleshooting starts by defining the symptom, scope, recent changes and impact. Change one variable at a time and record what you tried.","A laptop cannot open one website. Check whether other sites work, whether another device works, whether DNS resolves and whether the browser reports a certificate or connection error.","Write a troubleshooting ticket for a fictional slow laptop. Include user impact, device, symptoms, recent changes, checks and next action.","Why record the exact symptom before changing settings?","It creates a baseline and helps isolate the cause",["It makes the computer faster","It removes the need for testing"],"Good troubleshooting is evidence-led and reversible."],
["Operating systems, files and permissions","An operating system manages processes, memory, storage, devices and users. Filesystems organise data. Permissions control who can read, write or execute. Learn safe command-line navigation and least privilege.","A file marked read-only may be readable but not writable by a user. Changing permissions broadly can expose data; inspect ownership and required access first.","Practise navigating a local training folder, listing files, creating a directory and explaining owner/read/write/execute permissions.","What does least privilege mean?","Give only the access needed for the task",["Give everyone administrator access","Disable all passwords"],"Smaller permissions reduce the damage from mistakes or compromised accounts."],
["Networking fundamentals","A network moves data between devices using agreed protocols. Learn LAN/WAN, switches, routers, MAC and IP addresses, DNS, DHCP, TCP and UDP. Use layered models to locate where a failure may occur.","When you enter a domain name, DNS helps find an IP address. TCP can establish a reliable connection; HTTP or HTTPS then carries web requests at the application layer.","Draw a home network showing modem/router, Wi-Fi clients, switch (if present), DNS and internet. Label private vs public addressing.","What does DNS primarily do?","Maps domain names to network records such as IP addresses",["Encrypts every file","Assigns screen brightness"],"DNS resolution helps applications locate network services."],
["IPv4 addressing and subnetting","An IPv4 address has 32 bits. A CIDR prefix identifies the network bits; the remaining bits identify host space. Subnet masks and block sizes help calculate network boundaries. Special prefixes such as /31 and /32 have distinct uses.","For /24, the mask is 255.255.255.0. There are 256 addresses in the block; in an ordinary LAN, network and broadcast addresses are not assigned to hosts, leaving 254 conventional usable addresses.","Calculate network, broadcast and ordinary usable range for 192.168.10.77/26. Show the block size and verify the host lies inside the range.","How many total IPv4 addresses are in a /26 block?","64",["26","256"],"A /26 leaves 6 host bits, so 2^6 = 64 total addresses."],
["Cloud services, identity and access","Cloud services provide compute, storage, networking and managed applications. Learn IaaS/PaaS/SaaS, regions, availability, shared responsibility, identity, roles, MFA and secrets. Cloud security is not automatically handled by the provider.","A SaaS email provider manages the application platform, but the organisation still manages user accounts, MFA, sharing settings and appropriate data handling.","Design a fictional small team's cloud access matrix: role, resource, required action, approval and offboarding step. Do not create real cloud resources or expose credentials for this exercise.","What does shared responsibility mean?","Provider and customer each have defined security responsibilities",["The provider is responsible for everything","The customer is responsible for physical data centres"],"The split depends on the service model and configuration."],
["Defensive security and incident response","Security protects confidentiality, integrity and availability. Learn patching, backups, MFA, phishing awareness, endpoint protection, logging and incident response. Never scan, exploit or access systems without explicit authorisation.","If an account may be compromised, preserve evidence, report through the approved channel, revoke or reset credentials under policy, check MFA and review activity logs. Do not delete evidence or retaliate.","Write a tabletop response plan for a fictional phishing incident: detection, containment, reporting, recovery and lessons learned.","What is the first priority during a suspected security incident?","Follow the authorised response process and contain risk while preserving evidence",["Delete all logs","Post credentials publicly"],"Evidence and authorised containment matter more than improvisation."],
]},
maths:{title:"Mathematics, Science & Research Foundations",intro:"Refresh the quantitative foundations used across engineering, data, finance and technology. Start with units and arithmetic, then build algebra, statistics, calculus and evidence-based reasoning.",modules:[
["Numbers, units and estimation","Numbers describe quantities; units tell us what is being measured. Convert units before combining values. Estimate an answer first to catch mistakes. Significant figures communicate measurement precision.","2.5 km = 2,500 m because 1 km = 1,000 m. A speed of 60 km/h is 1 km/min; unit cancellation makes the conversion visible.","Convert 750 mL to litres and 2.4 hours to minutes. Estimate before calculating and show unit factors.","How many millilitres are in 1 litre?","1,000",["100","10"],"Metric prefixes are powers of ten; milli means one-thousandth."],
["Algebra, equations and graphs","A variable represents a value. An equation states equality. Solve by applying the same operation to both sides. A graph shows how variables relate; slope measures change in y per unit change in x for a straight line.","If 3x + 5 = 20, subtract 5 to get 3x = 15, then divide by 3: x = 5. Check by substitution: 3(5)+5=20.","Solve 4x − 7 = 13. Then plot y = 2x + 1 for x = 0, 1, 2 and describe the slope.","What is the solution of 4x − 7 = 13?","5",["4","6"],"Add 7 to both sides, then divide 20 by 4."],
["Ratios, percentages and financial maths","A ratio compares quantities. A percentage is a fraction out of 100. Compound growth applies growth to the updated balance each period; actual financial products may have fees, taxes and changing rates.","10% of ₹2,000 is ₹200. If a fictional balance grows 10% once, it becomes ₹2,200; a second 10% period applies to ₹2,200, not the original ₹2,000.","Calculate a 15% discount on ₹800 and compare simple vs two-period compound growth on a fictional ₹1,000 at 5% per period.","Why does compound growth differ from simple growth over multiple periods?","Each period's growth is applied to the updated balance",["It ignores the starting amount","It always uses a lower rate"],"Compounding includes prior growth in the base for the next period."],
["Probability and descriptive statistics","Probability quantifies uncertainty from 0 to 1. Mean is the arithmetic average; median is the middle sorted value; mode is the most frequent. Spread measures include range and standard deviation. Choose statistics based on distribution and question.","For 2, 3, 3, 4, 100, the mean is 22.4 while the median is 3. The outlier pulls the mean upward, so the median better represents a typical value in this small example.","Calculate mean, median and range for 4, 5, 5, 6, 30. Explain which summary is more representative and why.","Which statistic is more resistant to an extreme outlier?","Median",["Mean","Range"],"The median depends on the ordered middle value, not the magnitude of the outlier."],
["Functions, rates and calculus intuition","A function maps an input to an output. A derivative describes instantaneous rate of change; an integral accumulates quantities. Calculus models change, but the result is only as good as the assumptions and data.","If position is s(t)=3t² metres, velocity is ds/dt=6t m/s. At t=2 s, velocity is 12 m/s. The units confirm distance divided by time.","For s(t)=2t²+1, differentiate to find velocity and calculate it at t=3. State the units if s is metres and t seconds.","What does a derivative represent in a motion model?","Instantaneous rate of change of position (velocity)",["Total distance in all time","A constant unrelated to time"],"Velocity is the rate at which position changes with time."],
["Scientific method and evidence","A scientific investigation states a question, hypothesis, method, variables, observations and limitations. Correlation is not proof of causation. Reproducibility requires enough detail to repeat the method. Distinguish measurement, inference and opinion.","If two variables rise together, a third variable may influence both. A graph alone does not establish that one caused the other.","Design a safe desk-based investigation with one changed variable, one measured outcome, controls, repeated observations and a short limitations section.","What makes an experiment reproducible?","A sufficiently clear method, materials, conditions and recorded data",["A dramatic conclusion","A result with no method"],"Another learner should be able to repeat the procedure and compare outcomes."],
]},
creative:{title:"Creative Media, Design & Communication Foundations",intro:"Learn the fundamentals of visual communication, design systems, content production, storytelling and responsible publishing. Use original or properly licensed assets.",modules:[
["Visual communication and design principles","Design helps people notice, understand and act. Contrast creates hierarchy; alignment creates order; proximity groups related information; repetition builds consistency; whitespace reduces clutter. Design for the audience and task.","A poster with one clear headline, supporting detail and one action is easier to scan than a page where every element has equal size and colour.","Redesign a cluttered fictional event announcement using hierarchy, alignment, spacing and a clear call to action. Explain each choice.","What does visual hierarchy help a viewer do?","Understand the order and importance of information",["Make every element equally prominent","Hide the main message"],"Hierarchy guides attention and supports comprehension."],
["Colour, typography and layout systems","Colour has functional and cultural context; do not use colour alone to communicate status. Typography needs readable size, line length and contrast. A layout system uses spacing, grids and reusable components.","A status dashboard can pair a colour with text such as 'Delayed' and an icon, so the meaning is not lost for colour-blind users.","Create a mini style guide with type scale, spacing tokens, colour roles and examples of accessible status labels.","Why should colour not be the only way to communicate meaning?","Some users may not distinguish the colours or may use non-visual access",["It makes files larger","It prevents printing"],"Text labels and shapes provide redundant cues."],
["Branding and visual identity","A brand identity is a consistent system of name, voice, visual marks, colour and usage rules. A logo is one asset, not the whole brand. Research audience, competitors and context before designing.","A simple identity system might define a primary logo, clear-space rule, minimum size, two typefaces and colour values with contrast guidance.","Create a one-page identity board for a fictional student service. Include audience, positioning, logo concept, palette, typography and usage rules.","What should a brand guideline help others do?","Apply the identity consistently across different materials",["Choose a random new logo each day","Guarantee business success"],"Guidelines preserve coherence; they do not guarantee market outcomes."],
["Image, audio and video production","Production has pre-production, capture, editing and export stages. Plan a shot list or recording outline. Check resolution, aspect ratio, audio levels, captions and output format. Keep source files and versioned exports.","A 30-second explainer can use a hook, three visual steps and a closing action. Captions help viewers who cannot hear the audio.","Storyboard a short tutorial with six shots. Add a narration script, caption plan and export settings for mobile viewing.","What belongs in pre-production?","Brief, audience, script/storyboard and shot plan",["Only the final export","Deleting source files"],"Planning reduces avoidable reshoots and editing changes."],
["Storytelling and content strategy","A useful story has audience, purpose, context, evidence and a clear takeaway. A content plan defines format, cadence, source verification and feedback. Do not invent facts, misrepresent quotes or use copyrighted material without permission.","A factual explainer separates what is confirmed, what is alleged and what remains unknown, then links to primary sources.","Create a 60-second educational content outline with a hook, verified facts, source list, caveat and clear ending.","Why keep a source list for factual content?","So claims can be checked and corrected",["To make the video longer","To avoid explaining uncertainty"],"Traceable sources support accuracy and accountability."],
["Portfolio, licensing and professional delivery","A creative portfolio shows the brief, role, process, iterations, final work and reflection. Respect copyright, licences, releases, privacy and platform rules. Export accessible formats and retain editable source files.","A case study explains the original problem, constraints, sketches, feedback, final result and what changed. It does not claim team work as an individual contribution.","Package three original practice pieces with project briefs, process notes, asset licences, captions/alt text and a reflection.","What should you disclose when showing collaborative work?","Your own role and the contributions of others",["Claim all team work as yours","Hide the use of licensed assets"],"Honest attribution helps reviewers understand your actual contribution."],
]},
productivity:{title:"Personal Productivity & Knowledge Management",intro:"Build sustainable systems for planning, focus, note-taking, learning and workflow improvement. Tools should support priorities, not create busywork.",modules:[
["Goals, priorities and weekly planning","A goal describes an outcome; a task is an action. Break large goals into next actions, estimate effort and schedule realistic blocks. Review priorities when circumstances change.","Instead of 'learn Excel', define 'complete formulas lesson and build a SUMIFS tracker by Friday'. The second has a visible deliverable and deadline.","Choose one learning goal. Break it into five actions, estimate time and schedule a realistic week with buffer.","What makes a goal actionable?","A clear outcome, next action and time frame",["A vague wish with no next step","A list of unrelated tasks"],"Specific actions make progress observable."],
["Focus and attention management","Focused work reduces context switching. Use short planned sessions, remove avoidable distractions and take breaks. Match task difficulty to available energy. Do not treat a timer as a substitute for rest or health needs.","A 30-minute session can have one defined outcome: solve three SQL questions, check answers and write down one mistake to revisit.","Plan a 45-minute learning session with one goal, materials ready, distraction plan and a short review.","Why choose one clear outcome for a focus session?","It makes attention and completion easier to evaluate",["It guarantees no mistakes","It removes the need for breaks"],"A defined outcome limits task switching and vague activity."],
["Notes and knowledge capture","Good notes preserve useful ideas in your own words and link them to questions, examples and sources. Separate facts, assumptions and personal reflections. Keep notes searchable with consistent titles and tags.","A concept note can contain definition, worked example, common mistake, one question and source link. This is more useful than copying a full chapter.","Turn one lesson into a one-page note using definition, example, mistake, question and source.","What makes a note useful for later recall?","Clear structure, own words, examples and retrieval questions",["Copying every sentence without processing","A title with no content"],"Notes should help you retrieve and apply ideas."],
["Active recall and spaced practice","Active recall means retrieving information without looking at the answer. Spaced practice revisits material over increasing intervals. Interleave related problem types when useful and correct mistakes with explanations.","After studying subnetting, close the notes and calculate a new network range. Check the result, explain the error and retry later.","Create a 7-day review schedule for one topic with short recall questions and one cumulative problem.","What is the main benefit of active recall?","It strengthens retrieval and reveals gaps in understanding",["It makes reading unnecessary","It guarantees perfect memory"],"Retrieval practice tests whether knowledge can be used without prompts."],
["Digital organisation and information hygiene","Use clear file names, folders, version history and backups. Separate personal and public files. Use a password manager and MFA where possible. Avoid storing secrets in shared notes or public repositories.","A project folder can contain /docs, /data, /src and /exports, with README and dated releases. Keep private data outside a public repository.","Organise a fictional project folder, write a naming convention and create a backup/restore checklist.","Why keep a backup separate from the working copy?","To recover if the working copy is lost or corrupted",["To make duplicate files confusing","To publish private data"],"A backup should remain recoverable if the primary copy fails."],
["Workflow improvement and automation","A workflow is a repeatable sequence of steps. Map inputs, decisions, outputs and exceptions before automating. Automate stable repetitive tasks; keep human approval for consequential decisions and check failure paths.","Before automating reminders, identify the trigger, recipient, message, duplicate prevention, failure notification and owner.","Map one repetitive study or office task. Identify which steps are safe to automate and which need a human check.","What should happen before automating a process?","Understand the process, exceptions, risks and desired outcome",["Automate every step immediately","Remove all checks"],"Automating a poorly understood process can make errors happen faster."],
]}

};